/* ==============================================================
   01 — Your first program: getting the robot ready to move
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     Meca500 only (R3 or R4)
   TEACHES   What a .mxprog file is, and the four commands that
             take a robot from powered-on to ready for motion
   NEEDS     Robot powered on and connected in CONTROL mode.
             Nothing else — this file does the rest.
   WATCH     The robot homes itself, then makes one small move
   TIME      ~30 s including homing
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   Ϫ BEFORE YOU PRESS RUN

   This program HOMES the robot, which moves every joint. Clear
   the area around the arm and the gripper first. The manual is
   blunt about this and so are we: while homing, it is critical
   to remove any obstacles that could hinder the robot and
   gripper movements.

   If you are not sure the poses are clear on your setup, run it
   in simulation first — deactivate the robot and send
   ActivateSim(1). See the README.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THE WHOLE IDEA

   This file is a plain text file. Each line is one command. The
   robot reads them top to bottom and does them in that order.

   That is the entire programming model. There is no project
   file, no compile step, no runtime to install. What you type is
   what gets sent to the robot.

   WHAT THIS LANGUAGE DOES NOT HAVE

   No IF. No WHILE. No FOR. No jumps, no labels, no branching.

   That surprises people, so be ready for it. It is not a missing
   feature — it is the product decision that defines Mecademic.
   A Meca500 is a precision motion component, not a controller
   trying to be a PLC. The application logic — the state machine,
   the vision result, the recipe selection, the error recovery —
   lives in the customer's PC, IPC or PLC, in whatever language
   they already use. The robot is handed motion, and it executes
   motion beautifully.

   COMMENTS

   Comments are C/C++ style. This block you are reading is a
   block comment, opened with slash-star and closed with
   star-slash. A double slash comments to the end of the line.

   In the MecaPortal editor, Ctrl+/ toggles comments on whatever
   lines you have selected. Command names are case-insensitive,
   so SetWrf, SetWRF and setwrf all reach the same command — but
   pick one style and keep to it.

   UNITS

   Millimetres. Degrees. Seconds. Always, everywhere, with no
   setting to change and no way to get it wrong.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* ==============================================================
   PART 1 — MAKING THE ROBOT READY FOR MOTION
   ==============================================================

   A Meca500 that has just been powered on will refuse every
   motion command you send it. Five commands fix that, and they
   have to happen in this order.

   The useful property: ALL OF THEM ARE SAFE TO RUN EVERY TIME.
   Steps 1 to 4 are idempotent — none fails if the robot is
   already in the state it asks for, each just reports that there
   was nothing to do. Step 0 is the exception and is explained
   below. So this block can sit at the top of any program without
   you having to know what state the robot was left in, which is
   exactly why every example in this repo opens with it.

   Watch the log panel while this runs. Each command answers.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* 0. DROP EVERYTHING THE LAST PROGRAM LEFT BEHIND.
      Deactivating loses every non-persistent setting: tool and
      world frames, speeds, blending, payload, torque limits,
      posture and turn selection, move mode. They all return to
      firmware defaults when the robot comes back up.
      Answers [2004] Motors deactivated.

      This is the one command here that is NOT idempotent — it
      can only run when the robot is activated, and answers with
      an error if it is not. That is harmless: on a robot fresh
      from power-on there is nothing to deactivate and nothing
      to clear, so the error is expected and the rest of the file
      continues normally.

      It does not move the arm. It does NOT reset persistent
      settings — joint limits, work zone, tool sphere, collision
      and calibration config all survive. See
      common/cold_start.mxprog for those. */
DeactivateRobot()


/* 1. ENERGISE THE MOTORS.
      Activates all motors and releases the joint brakes.
      Answers [2000] Motors activated.

      Can be run in ANY robot state. If the robot is already
      activated, it answers and does nothing — which is why it is
      safe here unconditionally.

      Note: ActivateRobot(1) is different. The optional argument
      forces a re-initialisation of the drives, and homing is
      then required again. Use the bare form unless you mean it. */
ActivateRobot()


/* 2. FIND THE JOINT ZEROS.
      Homing establishes where the joints actually are. Until it
      completes, the robot's reported position is less accurate
      and motion is restricted.
      Answers [2002] Homing done.

      Also idempotent: if the robot is already homed, it answers
      and does nothing.

      If it fails you get [1032] — homing failed because joints
      are outside limits, meaning a joint is parked outside its
      USER-DEFINED limits and needs jogging back in — or [1014]
      for any other reason. */
Home()


/* 3. CLEAR ANY FAULT.
      Acknowledges and clears an error state left over from a
      previous run — a collision, a work zone breach, a torque
      limit trip.
      Answers [2005] if there was an error to reset, or [2006]
      if there was not. Both are fine.

      ORDER MATTERS HERE. ResetError can only run once the robot
      is "ready for motion", which means activated AND homed. So
      it goes after Home(), not before. A robot that errors while
      DEACTIVATED never enters an error state at all and needs no
      reset.

      Note that clearing a fault does not resume motion — that is
      the next command, and it is the step people forget. */
ResetError()


/* 4. RELEASE THE MOTION QUEUE.
      Resumes movement if it was paused: by ResetError above, by
      a PauseMotion command, or by a torque overload configured
      in pause-motion mode.
      Answers [2043] Motion resumed.

      Leaving this out is the classic "I cleared the error and it
      still will not move" problem. */
ResumeMotion()


/* --- The robot is now ready for motion -------------------------
   From here on, motion commands will be accepted.

   In the MecaPortal you would normally do all four of the above
   with the buttons in the toolbar. They are written out here
   because a host program has to send exactly these, in exactly
   this order, and because seeing them makes the robot's state
   model concrete.

   WHAT THIS DOES *NOT* DO

   Ready for motion is not the same as predictable. These four
   commands do not clear anything the previous user left behind —
   not a tool frame, not a restricted joint limit, not a work
   zone, not simulation mode.

   If you are on a robot you do not know, and you want the demo
   to behave the way it behaved when you rehearsed it, run
   common/cold_start.mxprog instead of this file. It deactivates
   and reactivates, which wipes every non-persistent setting in
   one stroke, and it audits the persistent ones that
   deactivation cannot touch.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* ==============================================================
   PART 2 — PUTTING THE SETTINGS IN A KNOWN STATE
   ============================================================== */

/* Being ready to move is not the same as being ready to run YOUR
   program. Speeds, reference frames, blending and posture are
   all still whatever the last program left behind.

   That is what the init helper is for. One line, and every
   setting this repo cares about is explicit:

       StartProgram("lib/init")

   It is commented out below because StartProgram only finds
   programs already SAVED ON THE ROBOT. Save common/init.mxprog
   as "lib/init" first, then uncomment. Read common/init.mxprog
   — it is the most reusable thing in this repo. */

// StartProgram("lib/init")

// Until you have saved it, set the two that matter here:
SetJointVel(15)
SetBlending(100)


/* ==============================================================
   PART 3 — A FIRST MOVE
   ============================================================== */

// Move to the zero position: all six joints at 0 degrees. This
// is the reference position marked on the robot body, with the
// flange pointing horizontally forward.
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)

// Rotate joint 1 by 30 degrees. Nothing else moves, because none
// of the other five numbers changed.
MoveJoints(30, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)

// Back to where we started.
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   ONE THING THAT IS NOT OBVIOUS: THE MOTION QUEUE

   The four readiness commands in Part 1 are ROBOT CONTROL
   commands: they execute immediately, the moment the robot
   receives them.

   The three moves in Part 3 are MOTION commands: they go into a
   MOTION QUEUE and are worked through in order, first in first
   out.

   So "the program finished" and "the robot stopped moving" are
   two different moments. All three moves can be sitting in the
   robot before the arm has finished the first one.

   This matters as soon as you coordinate with anything outside
   the robot — a gripper, a conveyor, a camera. The tool for that
   is SetCheckpoint(), covered in 07_delay_and_sequencing.mxprog.

   TRY THIS

   1. Select just the line MoveJoints(30, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0) and
      press Ctrl+Enter. Only that line runs. This is how to demo
      a single command without running the whole file, and it is
      the feature that makes the MecaPortal genuinely useful on
      a call.

   2. Run the whole file twice in a row. The second run is much
      quicker, because ActivateRobot and Home both find nothing
      to do and say so. Watch the log panel to see them answer
      without acting — that is what "safe to run every time"
      looks like.

   3. Force an error to see step 3 work: run
      MoveJoints(0, 0, 100, 0, 0, 0), which asks joint 3 past its
      70 degree limit. The robot refuses and goes into error.
      Now run this file again — ResetError clears it and
      ResumeMotion releases the queue, with no button pressing.

   NEXT   02_move_joints.mxprog — what those six numbers are.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
